**What triggered the automatic submission?**

The timer logic you now have in **`src/app/candidate/test/[id]/page.tsx`** intentionally calls `handleSubmit()` when the countdown reaches 0. The relevant code (lines 70‑74) is:

```tsx
useEffect(() => {
  if (!started) return;               // only runs after the test has started
  const interval = setInterval(() => {
    setTimeLeft(prev => {
      if (prev <= 1) {                // <-- when only 1 second is left
        clearInterval(interval);      // stop the timer
        handleSubmit();               // ⬅️ AUTO‑SUBMIT the assessment
        return 0;                     // reset the UI to “0:00”
      }
      return prev - 1;                // otherwise count down normally
    });
  }, 1000);
  return () => clearInterval(interval);
}, [started]);
```

**Why this happens**

1. **Timer start** – When the user clicks **Start Assessment**, `started` is set to `true`. The `useEffect` above then creates a 1‑second interval that decrements `timeLeft`.
2. **Zero‑second check** – As soon as `timeLeft` becomes `1` (i.e., the last second), the callback clears the interval and executes `handleSubmit()`.
3. **Result** – The assessment is submitted automatically without any further user interaction.

**How to change the behavior (if you don’t want auto‑submission)**  

- **Remove the auto‑submit call** – Delete or comment out the line `handleSubmit();` inside the `if (prev <= 1)` block.  
- **Add a confirmation step** – Instead of submitting directly, you could set a flag (e.g., `setTimeExpired(true)`) and show a modal asking the user to confirm submission. Then call `handleSubmit()` only after they confirm.  
- **Adjust the trigger** – If you prefer a grace period, change the condition to something like `if (prev <= 0)` (run after the timer displays “0:00”) or `if (prev < 0 && !submitted)` where `submitted` is a state flag that ensures the submit runs only once.

**Quick example to add a confirmation modal**

```tsx
const [timeExpired, setTimeExpired] = useState(false);

useEffect(() => {
  if (!started) return;
  const interval = setInterval(() => {
    setTimeLeft(prev => {
      if (prev <= 0) {
        clearInterval(interval);
        setTimeExpired(true);   // show confirm dialog
        return 0;
      }
      return prev - 1;
    });
  }, 1000);
  return () => clearInterval(interval);
}, [started]);

// In the JSX (e.g., after the timer UI)
{timeExpired && (
  <div className="fixed inset-0 bg-black/50 flex items-center justify-center">
    <div className="bg-white p-6 rounded-xl shadow-lg">
      <p className="mb-4 text-lg font-medium">Time is up! Submit your answers?</p>
      <button
        onClick={() => { handleSubmit(); setTimeExpired(false); }}
        className="px-4 py-2 bg-emerald-600 text-white rounded mr-2"
      >
        Submit
      </button>
      <button
        onClick={() => setTimeExpired(false)}
        className="px-4 py-2 bg-gray-300 text-gray-800 rounded"
      >
        Review
      </button>
    </div>
  </div>
)}
```

With this change, the assessment will **pause** when the timer expires and give the candidate a chance to review before the final submission.

---

**Bottom line:** The automatic submission you observed is the intended behavior of the countdown timer you added. If you’d like a different flow (no auto‑submit, a confirmation step, or a different timeout threshold), let me know and I can adjust the code accordingly.

